"SEA","distance":0.8613,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"LAC","distance":0.8772,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Falcons went 7-9 in 2018 and were outscored 423 to 414. The offense carried the team, ranking 8th in EPA per play while the defense finished 29th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were a neutral-script pass rate that was among the highest and the 3rd highest 2-Man rate in the league.","offense":"The Falcons offense was well above average by EPA per play, and it was much better passing the ball than running it. They threw more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score did not call for it. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 2nd highest singleback usage in the league. Matt Ryan ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Julio Jones, who drew 28% of the targets.","defense":"The Falcons had one of the worst defenses in the league, ranking 29th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Even its strongest area, pressure rate, only ranked 13th, and it was 30th in EPA allowed per rush. Takkarist McKinley led the team with 7 sacks, and Jack Crawford added 6 despite playing only 57% of the snaps. Schematically, they had the 3rd highest 2-Man rate in the league.","special":"The Falcons were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 96% of field goals, which was one of the best in the league. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Matt Ryan played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,924 yards. Julio Jones was the clear top target with 28% of the looks and 1,677 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Tevin Coleman and Ito Smith. On defense, Takkarist McKinley led the team with 7 sacks and Damontae Kazee picked off 7 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Vikings under Kevin O'Connell and the 2023 Cowboys under Mike McCarthy. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Eagles and the 2018 49ers under Robert Saleh.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the Falcons changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}